1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,147
Google Analytics and Google Tag Manager help you measure the visitors of your site.

2
00:00:06,147 --> 00:00:09,675
Orchard Core connects to each of them with a single ID.

3
00:00:09,675 --> 00:00:13,723
In this video, we enable the two features, save the IDs, and see

4
00:00:13,723 --> 00:00:15,400
where the scripts load.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,657
Orchard Core has two Google features for measuring visitors on public pages.

6
00:00:24,657 --> 00:00:29,086
Google Analytics takes the tracking ID of your Google Analytics account.

7
00:00:29,086 --> 00:00:33,800
Google Tag Manager takes the container ID of your Google Tag Manager account.

8
00:00:34,550 --> 00:00:38,788
Each feature adds its Google script to the head of every public page.

9
00:00:38,788 --> 00:00:43,026
With Tag Manager, you choose the tags in your Google account, and the

10
00:00:43,026 --> 00:00:45,230
site only needs the container ID.

11
00:00:45,980 --> 00:00:48,460
You can enable either feature or both.

12
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:52,518
Each has its own settings page, and nothing is added to your pages

13
00:00:52,518 --> 00:00:54,040
until an ID is saved.

14
00:00:58,252 --> 00:01:01,499
Neither feature is enabled until you turn it on.

15
00:01:01,499 --> 00:01:03,423
Open Tools, then Features.

16
00:01:03,423 --> 00:01:08,232
The page lists the features of the site by category, so search for Google.

17
00:01:08,709 --> 00:01:14,464
The Google category lists Google Analytics, Google Authentication, and Google Tag Manager.

18
00:01:14,464 --> 00:01:20,759
Google Authentication lets users sign in with their Google Account, and a separate video covers it.

19
00:01:21,212 --> 00:01:24,488
Let's select Google Analytics and Google Tag Manager.

20
00:01:24,488 --> 00:01:27,042
Each row also has its own Enable button.

21
00:01:27,498 --> 00:01:32,348
Then open Bulk Actions, and choose Enable to turn on both features together.

22
00:01:32,806 --> 00:01:36,046
A message confirms that both features are enabled.

23
00:01:40,277 --> 00:01:44,503
Open Settings, then Integrations, and select Google Analytics.

24
00:01:44,503 --> 00:01:50,097
Its heading reads Google Analytics Settings, with a See documentation link above it.

25
00:01:50,554 --> 00:01:54,304
It has one field, Tracking ID, and the field is required.

26
00:01:54,304 --> 00:01:59,244
The hint says it is the tracking ID defined in your Google Analytics account.

27
00:01:59,713 --> 00:02:03,793
Let's enter a sample ID, in place of the one from your own account.

28
00:02:04,255 --> 00:02:05,365
Select Save.

29
00:02:05,365 --> 00:02:09,005
The message reads Site settings updated successfully.

30
00:02:09,457 --> 00:02:15,627
Once the ID is saved, every public page of the site loads the Google Analytics script.

31
00:02:19,829 --> 00:02:22,739
Google Tag Manager works the same way.

32
00:02:22,739 --> 00:02:27,369
Open Settings, then Integrations, and select Google Tag Manager.

33
00:02:27,830 --> 00:02:33,818
The required field is Container ID, the container ID defined in your Google Tag Manager account.

34
00:02:33,818 --> 00:02:35,580
Let's enter a sample ID.

35
00:02:36,036 --> 00:02:36,970
Select Save.

36
00:02:36,970 --> 00:02:40,496
The same message confirms that the site settings were updated.

37
00:02:40,951 --> 00:02:46,389
Once the container ID is saved, public pages load the Tag Manager script as well.

38
00:02:46,389 --> 00:02:51,201
Each feature keeps its own ID, so you can change one without the other.

39
00:02:55,410 --> 00:02:58,038
Let's visit the home page as a visitor would.

40
00:02:58,038 --> 00:03:02,470
The page looks the same, because the scripts load from Google in the background.

41
00:03:02,923 --> 00:03:06,329
Every public page includes them, such as the About page.

42
00:03:06,329 --> 00:03:09,954
They are added each time a page is built, so the content you

43
00:03:09,954 --> 00:03:11,493
edit never holds them.

44
00:03:11,953 --> 00:03:14,420
Pages of the admin do not include them.

45
00:03:14,420 --> 00:03:17,928
This is the settings page we used, so your own work in the

46
00:03:17,928 --> 00:03:19,463
admin is not measured.

47
00:03:23,663 --> 00:03:26,057
Four cases leave a script out of a page.

48
00:03:26,057 --> 00:03:29,543
A page of the admin never has it, and neither does a response

49
00:03:29,543 --> 00:03:31,103
that is not a full page.

50
00:03:31,853 --> 00:03:34,874
A feature adds nothing until an ID is saved.

51
00:03:34,874 --> 00:03:40,493
And when the consent of the visitor does not allow tracking, both scripts are left out.

52
00:03:41,243 --> 00:03:45,502
To stop a script, disable its feature in Tools, Features.

53
00:03:45,502 --> 00:03:48,273
The ID field cannot be saved empty.

54
00:03:49,023 --> 00:03:54,140
The Administrator role has the permissions Manage Google Analytics settings and Manage Google

55
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:56,103
Tag Manager settings by default.

56
00:03:58,103 --> 00:04:02,173
That's Google Analytics and Google Tag Manager in Orchard Core.

57
00:04:02,173 --> 00:04:07,010
Enable a feature, save its ID, and your public pages load the Google script.

58
00:04:07,010 --> 00:04:08,543
Thanks for watching.

